llins's incumbency and financial edge keep this close, but the polling and market evidence points to a narrow Democratic edge.", "watch": "The remaining debates and early voting returns could shift a race this tight, and any polling error toward or against Collins would move the outcome.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Susan Collins (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Maine's Senate race is one of the closest contests of the cycle, with four-term incumbent Susan Collins defending her seat against Maine Senate President Troy Jackson in a state that has drifted Democratic in recent cycles. Polling through early October shows the two essentially tied: high-quality surveys from CBS/YouGov and InsiderAdvantage put the race dead even, while UNH and Fabrizio Ward give Jackson small leads and NYT/Siena gives Collins a slight edge. Averages across aggregators put Jackson up by roughly one to two points.\n\nPrediction markets lean Democratic, pricing Jackson between 53 and 58 percent, and several expert raters including the Economist, Silver Bulletin, and FPO call the race Lean D, though most rate it a Tossup. Collins has raised over $16 million and holds a large cash advantage, while Jackson has drawn high-profile reinforcement from Bernie Sanders on the campaign trail. With early voting underway and debates remaining, the race remains genuinely competitive.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7341, "margin": 3.16}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.6906, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.626, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.6915, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.5751, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.6364, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.6474, "margin": 2.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.6668, "margin": 2.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.6495, "margin": 2.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.6328, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.6994, "m